BUT... *early* in the game, the worst bad does not outweigh the best
good.

Because dying with a really early character is no real loss - if
you've only spent ten minutes on a character, it only takes another
ten minutes to get another one as good, and that is nothing. Whereas,
getting an early wish is a MAJOR gain - in fact, the earlier you get a
wish, the more major a gain it is, and the earlier you die, the less
of a loss it is.

This is why I always quaff at *all* fountains I see before at least
dungeon level 5. It doesn't matter whether I die to moccasins, to a
water demon, or to the later effects of having stuff cursed by a "This
Water's No Good" result - I haven't lost anything worth losing: but if
I get a wish, or even if I get a point of one of the non-exercisable
stats such as intelligence or charisma (or better still, both), it is
a better gain than the potential loss would have been.

And on at least one occasion, a "This Water's No Good" result managed
to curse a potion of water (to make it unholy), some scrolls of
teleportation and a scroll of genocide... hardly the worst of bad
news, as the other method of making unholy water (praying on the wrong
god's altar) is hardly the most advisable way of going about things.

